INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','easy','mcq','Which form of oral iron is most commonly used as first-line therapy for iron deficiency anaemia?','["A) Ferrous sulphate", "B) Ferric carboxymaltose", "C) Iron sucrose", "D) Ferric gluconate"]','A','Ferrous sulphate is the most widely used oral iron preparation due to its low cost, efficacy, and availability. Ferric carboxymaltose, iron sucrose, and ferric gluconate are all intravenous formulations used when oral iron is not tolerated or absorbed.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','medium','mcq','A patient with chronic kidney disease on haemodialysis develops iron deficiency anaemia despite oral iron supplementation. What is the most appropriate next step in iron therapy?','["A) Increase the dose of oral ferrous sulphate", "B) Switch to intravenous iron sucrose", "C) Add vitamin C to enhance absorption", "D) Start intramuscular iron dextran"]','B','In CKD patients on haemodialysis, oral iron absorption is poor and GI side effects are common; IV iron sucrose bypasses the gut and delivers iron directly into circulation. IM iron is rarely used due to pain and unpredictable absorption. Vitamin C supplementation alone is insufficient in this setting.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','medium','mcq','Which of the following is a recognised side effect of oral iron therapy that most commonly leads to non-compliance?','["A) Hypersensitivity reaction", "B) Thrombocytopenia", "C) Gastrointestinal upset and constipation", "D) Peripheral neuropathy"]','C','Gastrointestinal side effects — nausea, constipation, and dark stools — are the most common adverse effects of oral iron and the leading cause of non-compliance. Hypersensitivity is more associated with IV iron preparations. Iron does not typically cause thrombocytopenia or neuropathy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','hard','mcq','A 28-year-old woman with Crohn''s disease develops iron deficiency anaemia. She has failed two trials of oral iron due to GI intolerance and worsening of IBD symptoms. Which IV iron preparation is most appropriate?','["A) Iron dextran high-molecular weight", "B) Ferumoxytol", "C) Ferric carboxymaltose", "D) Oral ferrous fumarate with vitamin C"]','D','Ferric carboxymaltose is a preferred IV iron preparation in IBD because it can be given as a single large-dose infusion with a favourable safety profile. High-molecular-weight iron dextran carries the highest anaphylaxis risk. Oral iron is contraindicated in this patient due to documented intolerance and IBD exacerbation.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','easy','mcq','Which laboratory parameter rises earliest and is most useful for confirming a response to iron therapy within 7-10 days of starting treatment?','["A) Reticulocyte count", "B) Serum ferritin", "C) Mean corpuscular volume", "D) Total iron-binding capacity"]','A','Reticulocyte count rises within 7-10 days of starting iron therapy, reflecting increased erythropoiesis, and is the earliest indicator of response. Serum ferritin, MCV, and TIBC change later, reflecting replenishment of stores or structural red cell changes over weeks to months.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','medium','mcq','Which of the following medications significantly reduces oral iron absorption when taken concomitantly?','["A) Ascorbic acid", "B) Proton pump inhibitors", "C) Metformin", "D) Folic acid"]','B','Proton pump inhibitors raise gastric pH, reducing ferric iron solubility and significantly impairing absorption, which depends on an acidic environment to convert Fe3+ to the more readily absorbed Fe2+ form. Ascorbic acid enhances iron absorption. Metformin and folic acid do not significantly affect iron absorption.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','hard','mcq','A patient receiving IV iron dextran develops acute hypotension, urticaria, and bronchospasm within minutes of infusion. What is the most appropriate immediate management?','["A) Slow the infusion rate and give paracetamol", "B) Stop infusion and administer IV hydrocortisone only", "C) Stop infusion immediately and administer IV adrenaline with IV fluids", "D) Switch to oral iron and observe"]','C','Anaphylaxis to IV iron dextran requires immediate cessation of infusion followed by IV adrenaline (epinephrine) as first-line treatment, with IV fluids for haemodynamic support. Paracetamol and slowing the infusion are insufficient for true anaphylaxis. Hydrocortisone alone is inadequate as first-line treatment for anaphylaxis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','medium','mcq','How long should oral iron therapy typically be continued after haemoglobin levels have normalised in iron deficiency anaemia?','["A) Stop immediately once Hb normalises", "B) Continue for 1 week", "C) Continue for 1 month only", "D) Continue for 3-6 months to replenish iron stores"]','D','Iron therapy should be continued for 3-6 months after haemoglobin normalisation to replenish depleted iron stores, reflected by a return of serum ferritin to normal. Stopping at Hb normalisation leaves stores depleted and risks early recurrence of iron deficiency anaemia.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','easy','mcq','Oral iron is best absorbed when taken in what manner?','["A) On an empty stomach or with vitamin C (ascorbic acid)", "B) With meals and milk", "C) With calcium supplements", "D) At bedtime with antacids"]','A','Oral iron is best absorbed on an empty stomach in an acidic environment; vitamin C (ascorbic acid) enhances absorption by keeping iron in the ferrous (Fe2+) state. Food, milk, calcium, and antacids all impair iron absorption by raising gastric pH or forming insoluble complexes with iron.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Iron Therapy','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old man on haemodialysis is prescribed IV iron sucrose. Which statement regarding iron sucrose administration is correct?','["A) It requires a test dose before each administration", "B) It is administered in small divided doses (100-200 mg per session) due to its pharmacokinetic profile", "C) It can be given as a single large 1000 mg infusion like ferric carboxymaltose", "D) It carries the highest risk of anaphylaxis among all IV iron preparations"]','B','Iron sucrose is typically given in divided doses of 100-200 mg per dialysis session due to its shorter half-life and to minimise adverse effects. It does not require a test dose. Ferric carboxymaltose, not iron sucrose, can be given as a single large-dose infusion. High-molecular-weight iron dextran carries the highest anaphylaxis risk.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','medium','mcq','Which form of vitamin B12 is preferred for intramuscular injection in the treatment of pernicious anaemia in the UK?','["A) Methylcobalamin oral tablets", "B) Cyanocobalamin sublingual spray", "C) Hydroxocobalamin IM injection", "D) Cyanocobalamin IV infusion"]','C','Hydroxocobalamin is preferred for IM injection in pernicious anaemia because it is retained in the body longer than cyanocobalamin, requiring less frequent maintenance dosing (every 3 months vs every 2 months). In pernicious anaemia, intrinsic factor is absent so standard-dose oral B12 cannot be absorbed via the normal pathway.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','hard','mcq','A 65-year-old vegan woman presents with macrocytic anaemia and peripheral neuropathy. Serum B12 is very low and intrinsic factor antibodies are negative. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?','["A) High-dose oral folic acid 5 mg daily alone", "B) Dietary advice and increased B12-rich food intake", "C) Oral ferrous sulphate for suspected concurrent iron deficiency", "D) Intramuscular hydroxocobalamin injections"]','D','Neurological complications of B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord) require prompt IM hydroxocobalamin to ensure adequate tissue levels and prevent irreversible neurological damage. Folic acid alone can mask haematological signs while neurological damage progresses. Dietary advice and oral iron do not address the underlying B12 deficiency.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','easy','mcq','Which of the following is the most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency in elderly patients?','["A) Pernicious anaemia (autoimmune lack of intrinsic factor)", "B) Strict vegetarian diet", "C) Malabsorption due to coeliac disease", "D) Metformin use"]','A','Pernicious anaemia, caused by autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells leading to lack of intrinsic factor and B12 malabsorption, is the most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency in the elderly. Dietary deficiency is more common in strict vegans. Metformin reduces B12 absorption but is a less common cause of clinical deficiency.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','medium','mcq','Why must folic acid supplementation NOT be given alone in a patient with combined B12 and folate deficiency?','["A) Folic acid increases the risk of arterial thrombosis", "B) Folic acid corrects the anaemia but allows neurological damage from B12 deficiency to progress undetected", "C) Folic acid worsens the macrocytosis", "D) Folic acid blocks B12 absorption from the gut"]','B','Folic acid can correct the haematological manifestations of B12 deficiency, masking the diagnosis while subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord continues to progress irreversibly. This is why B12 must always be replaced simultaneously when co-deficiency exists. Folic acid does not worsen macrocytosis, cause thrombosis, or block B12 absorption.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','easy','mcq','High-dose folic acid 5 mg daily is recommended periconceptually and in early pregnancy primarily to prevent which congenital abnormality?','["A) Ventricular septal defect", "B) Down syndrome", "C) Neural tube defects (e.g., spina bifida, anencephaly)", "D) Cleft palate"]','C','Folic acid supplementation (400 mcg daily for standard risk; 5 mg daily for high risk) before conception and during the first 12 weeks of pregnancy significantly reduces the risk of neural tube defects such as spina bifida and anencephaly. It does not prevent chromosomal abnormalities (Down syndrome) or cardiac structural defects.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','medium','mcq','Which drug commonly used in clinical practice causes folate deficiency through inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase?','["A) Metronidazole", "B) Metformin", "C) Methyldopa", "D) Methotrexate"]','D','Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), the enzyme responsible for converting dietary folate to its active tetrahydrofolate form, thereby depleting folate stores and causing megaloblastic changes. This is why folinic acid (leucovorin) rescue is given after high-dose methotrexate. Metformin reduces B12, not folate.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','hard','mcq','A patient with pernicious anaemia on IM hydroxocobalamin every 3 months has difficulty attending clinic. Which alternative can be considered for a highly adherent patient?','["A) High-dose oral cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg daily", "B) Weekly IM cyanocobalamin injections self-administered", "C) Dietary B12 supplementation alone", "D) Sublingual folic acid 5 mg daily"]','A','High-dose oral cyanocobalamin (1000-2000 mcg daily) can achieve adequate serum B12 via mass-action passive diffusion (approximately 1% absorbed without intrinsic factor), making it a viable alternative in adherent patients. IM dosing remains the gold standard for reliability. Folic acid does not replace B12, and dietary sources cannot compensate for absent intrinsic factor at standard intake.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','medium','mcq','Which peripheral blood film finding is most characteristic of vitamin B12 or folate deficiency?','["A) Microcytic hypochromic red cells", "B) Hypersegmented neutrophils and oval macrocytes", "C) Target cells and acanthocytes", "D) Spherocytes and fragmented red cells"]','B','Hypersegmented neutrophils (5 or more lobes) and oval macrocytes are the hallmark peripheral blood smear findings in megaloblastic anaemia due to B12 or folate deficiency. Microcytic hypochromic cells suggest iron deficiency; target cells suggest liver disease or thalassaemia; spherocytes indicate haemolytic anaemia.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','medium','mcq','A patient on phenytoin for epilepsy develops macrocytic anaemia with a low serum folate. Which mechanism best explains this finding?','["A) Phenytoin causes autoimmune haemolysis depleting red cells", "B) Phenytoin blocks vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum", "C) Phenytoin impairs folate absorption and may accelerate its catabolism via hepatic enzyme induction", "D) Phenytoin inhibits erythropoietin secretion from the kidney"]','C','Phenytoin and other anticonvulsants can impair folate absorption from the gut and may accelerate its hepatic catabolism via CYP enzyme induction, resulting in folate deficiency and megaloblastic anaemia. They do not significantly block B12 absorption or inhibit erythropoietin secretion. Folic acid supplementation is often recommended alongside long-term anticonvulsant therapy.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','B12 & Folate Supplements','easy','mcq','Which route of B12 administration completely bypasses the requirement for intrinsic factor?','["A) Standard-dose oral tablets (e.g., 10 mcg daily)", "B) Dietary B12 from animal products", "C) Sublingual standard-dose spray", "D) Intramuscular injection of hydroxocobalamin"]','D','Intramuscular injection delivers B12 directly into systemic circulation, completely bypassing the intrinsic factor-dependent ileal absorption pathway. Standard-dose oral and dietary B12 cannot be absorbed without intrinsic factor. High-dose oral or sublingual B12 can exploit passive diffusion but this is less reliable than IM injection.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','easy','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of erythropoietin (EPO) as an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent?','["A) Inhibits apoptosis of erythroid progenitor cells and stimulates their proliferation and differentiation in bone marrow", "B) Directly stimulates haemoglobin synthesis in circulating mature red blood cells", "C) Increases intestinal iron absorption to support red cell production", "D) Activates thrombopoietin receptors to enhance platelet and red cell production"]','A','Erythropoietin binds to specific receptors on erythroid progenitor cells (BFU-E and CFU-E) in the bone marrow, inhibiting their apoptosis and promoting proliferation and differentiation into mature red blood cells. It does not directly synthesise haemoglobin, increase iron absorption, or affect platelet production.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','medium','mcq','Compared to epoetin alfa, what is the main pharmacokinetic advantage of darbepoetin alfa?','["A) It does not require concurrent iron supplementation", "B) It has a longer serum half-life allowing weekly or biweekly dosing instead of three times weekly", "C) It is orally bioavailable unlike epoetin alfa", "D) It lacks the risk of hypertension that epoetin alfa carries"]','B','Darbepoetin alfa has a longer serum half-life (approximately 25 hours IV vs 8 hours for epoetin alfa) due to additional N-linked carbohydrate chains that reduce renal clearance, allowing weekly or biweekly dosing versus three times weekly for epoetin. Both agents carry risks of hypertension and require iron supplementation, and neither is orally bioavailable.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','medium','mcq','Which is the most serious cardiovascular risk associated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents when haemoglobin targets are allowed to exceed 12 g/dL?','["A) Atrial fibrillation and cardiac conduction defects", "B) Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade", "C) Thromboembolic events including stroke, MI, and DVT", "D) Symptomatic bradycardia"]','C','ESAs at supratherapeutic haemoglobin targets increase blood viscosity and stimulate vascular proliferation, leading to significantly increased risk of thromboembolic events including DVT, stroke, and myocardial infarction. Clinical guidelines therefore target Hb 10-11 g/dL in CKD to minimise this risk. Atrial fibrillation and bradycardia are not characteristic ESA adverse effects.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','hard','mcq','A patient with non-small cell lung cancer receiving chemotherapy is prescribed epoetin alfa for chemotherapy-induced anaemia. What is the major oncological concern with ESA use in this setting?','["A) Epoetin alfa significantly worsens chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting", "B) ESAs reduce the cytotoxic efficacy of platinum-based chemotherapy agents", "C) ESAs cause severe neutropenia that increases infection risk in cancer patients", "D) ESAs may stimulate tumour growth via erythropoietin receptors on tumour cells, potentially reducing overall survival"]','D','Clinical trials have shown that ESAs may stimulate erythropoietin receptors expressed on tumour cells, potentially promoting tumour progression and reducing overall survival in some cancer types. For this reason, ESA use in cancer patients is restricted to chemotherapy-induced anaemia only, targeting the lowest Hb needed to avoid transfusion, and is not recommended when chemotherapy is given with curative intent.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','easy','mcq','Before initiating ESA therapy, which nutritional deficiency must be corrected to ensure an adequate erythropoietic response?','["A) Iron deficiency", "B) Vitamin D deficiency", "C) Magnesium deficiency", "D) Zinc deficiency"]','A','Iron deficiency must be corrected before or alongside ESA therapy because ESAs drive rapid erythropoiesis that rapidly consumes available iron stores; insufficient iron leads to functional iron deficiency and a blunted haematological response to the ESA. Vitamin D, magnesium, and zinc deficiencies do not significantly limit erythropoiesis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','medium','mcq','A patient on subcutaneous epoetin alfa develops sudden worsening anaemia with reticulocyte count near zero. Anti-EPO antibodies are detected. What is this condition called?','["A) Aplastic anaemia from bone marrow suppression by EPO", "B) Pure red cell aplasia (PRCA) due to anti-erythropoietin neutralising antibodies", "C) Immune-mediated haemolytic anaemia triggered by ESA therapy", "D) Folate depletion anaemia from rapid erythropoiesis consuming folate stores"]','B','ESA-associated PRCA is caused by the development of anti-erythropoietin neutralising antibodies that cross-react with endogenous EPO, leading to profound anaemia with near-absent reticulocytes and absent erythroid precursors in the bone marrow. Management includes immediately stopping the ESA and starting immunosuppression. Folate depletion causes megaloblastic changes, not aplasia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','hard','mcq','A CKD patient on haemodialysis has been on adequate ESA doses but shows persistently poor haematological response. What is the most common cause of ESA hyporesponsiveness?','["A) The ESA dose is too high, causing receptor downregulation", "B) Concomitant use of ACE inhibitors blunting the EPO response", "C) Absolute or functional iron deficiency, or underlying chronic inflammation (elevated hepcidin)", "D) Excessive vitamin B6 supplementation inhibiting erythropoiesis"]','C','The most common causes of ESA hyporesponsiveness are absolute or functional iron deficiency and chronic inflammation (which drives hepcidin elevation, causing iron sequestration and limiting iron availability for erythropoiesis). ACE inhibitors may mildly blunt EPO response but are not the most common cause. Vitamin B6 excess does not impair erythropoiesis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','medium','mcq','Epoetin alfa is approved for anaemia management in which of the following conditions?','["A) Iron deficiency anaemia in otherwise healthy non-dialysis patients", "B) Sickle cell disease-related haemolytic anaemia", "C) Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (AIHA)", "D) Anaemia of CKD, anaemia in HIV patients on zidovudine, and chemotherapy-induced anaemia"]','D','Epoetin alfa is approved for anaemia of CKD (dialysis and non-dialysis), anaemia in HIV patients on zidovudine (which suppresses erythropoiesis), and chemotherapy-induced anaemia in selected patients. It is not indicated for iron deficiency anaemia, sickle cell disease, or autoimmune haemolytic anaemia where other mechanisms are primary.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','easy','mcq','Which cardiovascular adverse effect of ESA therapy should be actively monitored at every clinic visit?','["A) Hypertension", "B) Hypotension", "C) Hypoglycaemia", "D) Hypothyroidism"]','A','Hypertension is a common and well-recognised adverse effect of ESA therapy, occurring in up to 30% of patients, likely due to increased blood viscosity, vasoconstriction from elevated haemoglobin, and direct ESA effects on vascular tone. Blood pressure should be monitored at every clinic visit and managed with antihypertensives if needed.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents','medium','mcq','Which statement about methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta (MIRCERA) is correct?','["A) It must be given three times weekly like standard epoetin alfa", "B) It is a continuous erythropoietin receptor activator with a half-life of approximately 130 hours, enabling once-monthly dosing", "C) It is indicated specifically for chemotherapy-induced anaemia and not CKD", "D) It carries no risk of thromboembolism compared to other ESAs due to its unique molecular structure"]','B','Methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta (MIRCERA) is a continuous erythropoietin receptor activator with a prolonged half-life of approximately 130 hours due to PEGylation, enabling once-monthly subcutaneous or IV dosing in stable CKD patients. All ESAs share class-related risks including thromboembolism and hypertension regardless of molecular structure.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','medium','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of unfractionated heparin (UFH)?','["A) Inhibits vitamin K-dependent clotting factor synthesis in the liver", "B) Directly inhibits thrombin by binding to its active catalytic site", "C) Binds antithrombin III, causing a conformational change that accelerates its inhibitory effect on thrombin and factor Xa approximately 1000-fold", "D) Blocks fibrinogen conversion to fibrin by inhibiting thrombins fibrinogenolytic site"]','C','UFH binds to antithrombin III (ATIII), inducing a conformational change that dramatically accelerates ATIIIs inhibitory activity against thrombin (factor IIa) and factor Xa. The pentasaccharide sequence within heparin binds ATIII; additional longer chain sequences are required to also inhibit thrombin. Warfarin inhibits vitamin K-dependent factor synthesis; direct thrombin inhibitors like bivalirudin bind thrombins active site directly.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','hard','mcq','Which monitoring parameter is used to guide unfractionated heparin therapy, and what is the therapeutic target range?','["A) INR; target 2.0-3.0", "B) Anti-Xa level; target 0.6-1.0 IU/mL", "C) Platelet count; target above 150 × 10 to the power of 9 per litre", "D) Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT); target 1.5-2.5 times the control value (approximately 60-100 seconds)"]','D','The aPTT is the standard monitoring tool for UFH, with a therapeutic range of 1.5-2.5 times the laboratory control value. INR monitors warfarin therapy; anti-Xa monitoring is used for LMWH in special populations such as renal impairment and pregnancy; platelet count is monitored to detect HIT but is not a therapeutic efficacy target.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','easy','mcq','Which agent is used to reverse the anticoagulant effect of unfractionated heparin in the event of serious bleeding?','["A) Protamine sulphate", "B) Vitamin K", "C) Idarucizumab", "D) Andexanet alfa"]','A','Protamine sulphate is a positively charged protein that binds and neutralises negatively charged heparin, rapidly reversing its anticoagulant effect. Vitamin K reverses warfarin; idarucizumab reverses dabigatran; andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors such as rivaroxaban and apixaban.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','medium','mcq','A patient develops a falling platelet count 7 days after starting UFH for DVT. Platelets drop from 250 to 80 × 10 to the power of 9 per litre. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate action?','["A) Spontaneous thrombocytopenia — continue heparin and monitor platelet count", "B) Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type II (HIT) — stop all heparin immediately and start a non-heparin anticoagulant", "C) Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia — stop heparin and start warfarin immediately", "D) Drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia — administer intravenous immunoglobulin"]','B','Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia type II (HIT) classically presents with a 50% or greater platelet fall 5-10 days after heparin initiation due to IgG antibodies against the heparin-PF4 complex. All heparin must be stopped immediately and a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban, fondaparinux, or danaparoid) started due to paradoxical thrombosis risk. Warfarin must not be started until platelets recover, as it risks warfarin-induced skin necrosis in HIT.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','medium','mcq','What key pharmacokinetic advantage do low-molecular-weight heparins (LMWHs) have over unfractionated heparin?','["A) LMWHs can be completely reversed with protamine sulphate", "B) LMWHs are metabolised hepatically unlike UFH which is renally cleared", "C) LMWHs have predictable pharmacokinetics due to less plasma protein binding, allowing weight-based dosing without routine monitoring in most patients", "D) LMWHs cross the placenta and provide fetal anticoagulation in pregnancy"]','C','LMWHs (e.g., enoxaparin, dalteparin) have more predictable dose-response pharmacokinetics due to less plasma protein and endothelial cell binding, and a more consistent anti-Xa to anti-IIa ratio, allowing weight-based dosing without routine monitoring in most patients. Protamine only partially reverses LMWHs. LMWHs do NOT cross the placenta, making them safe in pregnancy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','hard','mcq','In which patient population should LMWH dose be adjusted or anti-Xa monitoring used, and why?','["A) Obese patients over 120 kg, due to increased hepatic metabolism", "B) Patients with mild hepatic impairment, because LMWHs are hepatically metabolised", "C) Elderly patients over 70, because of increased oral bioavailability with age", "D) Patients with severe renal impairment (eGFR below 30 mL/min), because LMWHs accumulate due to renal excretion and increase bleeding risk"]','D','LMWHs are primarily renally excreted and accumulate significantly in severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min), markedly increasing haemorrhagic risk. In these patients, unfractionated heparin (which is not renally cleared) is preferred, or dose-reduced LMWH with anti-Xa monitoring is used. LMWHs are not hepatically metabolised and have no oral bioavailability.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','medium','mcq','Heparin and LMWHs are the anticoagulants of choice in pregnancy. Why is warfarin avoided, particularly in the first trimester?','["A) Warfarin crosses the placenta and is teratogenic (warfarin embryopathy) in the first trimester and risks fetal haemorrhage throughout pregnancy", "B) Warfarin causes heparin-induced thrombocytopenia in neonates", "C) Warfarin is excreted into breast milk causing neonatal anticoagulation", "D) Warfarin increases the risk of placental abruption by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis"]','A','Warfarin is a small lipophilic molecule that crosses the placenta and inhibits fetal vitamin K-dependent clotting factors. In the first trimester (6-12 weeks) it causes warfarin embryopathy (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses), and in any trimester it risks fetal or neonatal haemorrhage. Heparin and LMWHs do not cross the placenta, making them safe throughout pregnancy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','easy','mcq','A 70 kg patient with acute pulmonary embolism requires intravenous UFH. Using the standard weight-based protocol, what is the correct initial bolus dose?','["A) 3500 units IV", "B) 5600 units IV", "C) 8000 units IV", "D) 10000 units IV"]','B','The standard UFH loading dose for VTE treatment is 80 units/kg IV bolus: 80 units per kg multiplied by 70 kg equals 5600 units. The maintenance infusion is then typically started at 18 units/kg/hour and adjusted according to aPTT results. Lower doses (e.g., 5000 units SC twice or three times daily) are used for prophylaxis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','hard','mcq','Which of the following statements about fondaparinux is correct?','["A) Fondaparinux is a low-molecular-weight heparin that inhibits both thrombin and factor Xa", "B) Fondaparinux can be reversed with protamine sulphate in case of significant bleeding", "C) Fondaparinux is a synthetic pentasaccharide that selectively inhibits factor Xa via antithrombin and can be used in HIT as it does not bind PF4", "D) Fondaparinux is contraindicated in patients with a history of HIT"]','C','Fondaparinux is a synthetic pentasaccharide that binds antithrombin to selectively inhibit factor Xa only (no anti-IIa activity). It cannot be reversed by protamine. Crucially, fondaparinux does not bind platelet factor 4 and therefore does not cause HIT, making it a suitable alternative anticoagulant in HIT patients. It is not an LMWH.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Heparin & LMWHs','medium','mcq','What is the mechanism by which UFH prevents clot propagation differently from LMWHs?','["A) UFH inhibits only factor Xa, while LMWH inhibits only thrombin", "B) UFH directly lyses existing thrombus, whereas LMWH only prevents new clot formation", "C) UFH and LMWH have identical mechanisms and differ only in molecular size", "D) UFH inhibits both thrombin (factor IIa) and factor Xa, while LMWHs predominantly inhibit factor Xa with less anti-IIa activity due to their shorter chain length"]','D','UFH chains are long enough to simultaneously bridge antithrombin to both factor Xa and thrombin, inhibiting both. LMWHs are too short to bridge antithrombin to thrombin, so they predominantly inhibit factor Xa. This gives LMWHs a more predictable anti-Xa to anti-IIa ratio (typically 2:1 to 4:1) compared to UFH (approximately 1:1). Neither drug lyses existing thrombus.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','easy','mcq','What is the mechanism of action of warfarin?','["A) Inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), blocking regeneration of active vitamin K and reducing synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X", "B) Directly inhibits thrombin by binding its active catalytic site", "C) Activates antithrombin III to inhibit factor Xa selectively", "D) Irreversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 to block thromboxane A2 production"]','A','Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), preventing the recycling of vitamin K to its active reduced form, which is required as a cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X and anticoagulant proteins C and S. Direct thrombin inhibition describes dabigatran; ATIII activation describes heparin; COX-1 inhibition describes aspirin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','medium','mcq','A patient stabilised on warfarin with an INR of 2.5 is started on rifampicin for tuberculosis. What change in INR is expected?','["A) INR will increase because rifampicin inhibits CYP2C9 reducing warfarin metabolism", "B) INR will decrease because rifampicin induces CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, dramatically accelerating warfarin metabolism", "C) INR will remain unchanged as rifampicin does not interact with warfarin significantly", "D) INR will fluctuate unpredictably due to rifampicin displacing warfarin from plasma proteins"]','B','Rifampicin is a potent inducer of CYP2C9, CYP3A4, and P-glycoprotein, dramatically increasing warfarin metabolism and reducing its plasma levels, leading to a fall in INR and loss of anticoagulation. The warfarin dose must be substantially increased with close monitoring. Enzyme inhibitors (e.g., fluconazole, amiodarone) would raise the INR.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','medium','mcq','A patient on warfarin presents with an INR of 8.5 and minor gum bleeding only. There is no major haemorrhage. What is the most appropriate management?','["A) Continue warfarin at the same dose and recheck INR in 1 week", "B) Give fresh frozen plasma (FFP) immediately to normalise INR", "C) Withhold warfarin temporarily and give low-dose oral vitamin K 1-5 mg; recheck INR in 24 hours", "D) Give intravenous vitamin K 10 mg to achieve rapid and sustained reversal"]','C','For supratherapeutic INR (5-10) with only minor bleeding, guidelines recommend withholding warfarin and giving low-dose oral vitamin K (1-5 mg), which gently corrects the INR over 24-48 hours without causing prolonged warfarin resistance. FFP and high-dose IV vitamin K are reserved for major or life-threatening bleeding because high-dose IV vitamin K causes warfarin resistance for weeks.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','hard','mcq','A patient on warfarin presents with acute intracranial haemorrhage and an INR of 4.2. What is the fastest approach to reverse warfarin anticoagulation?','["A) High-dose intravenous vitamin K 10 mg alone — fastest available reversal", "B) Oral vitamin K 10 mg plus fresh frozen plasma infusion", "C) Platelet transfusion plus cryoprecipitate", "D) Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) plus intravenous vitamin K"]','D','For life-threatening warfarin-associated bleeding such as intracranial haemorrhage, four-factor PCC provides immediate replacement of factors II, VII, IX, and X in a small volume and achieves the fastest INR normalisation. IV vitamin K must be given concurrently to sustain reversal as PCC factors are consumed. FFP requires large volumes, takes longer to work, and risks volume overload. Platelets and cryoprecipitate do not address factor deficiency.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','easy','mcq','Which vitamin K-dependent clotting factor has the shortest half-life and is responsible for the early rise in INR after starting warfarin?','["A) Factor VII", "B) Factor II (prothrombin)", "C) Factor IX", "D) Factor X"]','A','Factor VII has the shortest half-life (approximately 6 hours) of all vitamin K-dependent clotting factors, so its levels fall first after warfarin initiation, causing an early rise in the PT/INR. However, this early INR rise does not reflect full anticoagulation because the more clinically important factors II, IX, and X (longer half-lives) are not yet depleted.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','medium','mcq','Which of the following drugs increases the anticoagulant effect of warfarin by inhibiting CYP2C9-mediated warfarin metabolism?','["A) Rifampicin", "B) Amiodarone", "C) Carbamazepine", "D) St Johns Wort"]','B','Amiodarone is a potent inhibitor of CYP2C9 (and CYP3A4), substantially reducing metabolism of the more potent S-enantiomer of warfarin, leading to markedly elevated warfarin levels and raised INR with significant bleeding risk. Rifampicin, carbamazepine, and St Johns Wort are all CYP inducers that decrease warfarin levels and reduce INR.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','hard','mcq','Why does warfarin initiation create a paradoxical transient hypercoagulable state in the first 2-3 days?','["A) Warfarin transiently activates platelet aggregation through thromboxane A2 upregulation", "B) Warfarin stimulates a rebound surge in fibrinogen synthesis during the first 48 hours", "C) Protein C and Protein S (anticoagulant proteins) have shorter half-lives than procoagulant factors and are depleted first, creating a temporary prothrombotic imbalance", "D) Warfarin induces a rebound increase in factor VIII through von Willebrand factor release"]','C','Protein C and Protein S are vitamin K-dependent anticoagulant proteins with short half-lives (Protein C approximately 8 hours, Protein S approximately 30 hours). They are depleted before the procoagulant factors II, IX, and X (which have longer half-lives), creating a transient hypercoagulable state in the first 2-3 days. This is why warfarin must be bridged with heparin in high-risk patients at initiation.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','medium','mcq','A patient with a mechanical heart valve requires anticoagulation with warfarin (target INR 2.5-3.5). Regarding monitoring frequency once stable, which statement is correct?','["A) INR must be checked daily even once stabilised on warfarin", "B) Warfarin is monitored using the aPTT rather than INR in patients with mechanical valves", "C) INR testing is unnecessary once a stable dose is established for more than 3 months", "D) Once stable within therapeutic range, INR monitoring frequency can be extended to every 4-12 weeks"]','D','Once warfarin is stabilised within therapeutic range, monitoring can be safely reduced to every 4-12 weeks in compliant patients with stable diet and medications. INR is measured by the prothrombin time (expressed as INR), not aPTT. INR monitoring must never be abandoned as diet changes, illness, and drug interactions constantly alter INR.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','easy','mcq','A patient on warfarin is started on co-trimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) for a urinary tract infection. What effect on INR would you expect?','["A) INR rises due to co-trimoxazole inhibiting CYP2C9 and reducing warfarin metabolism", "B) INR falls due to enzyme induction by co-trimoxazole", "C) No significant change as antibiotics do not affect warfarin metabolism", "D) INR falls due to co-trimoxazole increasing fibrinogen synthesis"]','A','Co-trimoxazole inhibits CYP2C9, reducing warfarin metabolism and increasing warfarin plasma levels, thereby raising the INR and bleeding risk. Many antibiotics also reduce gut flora that produce vitamin K2, contributing to INR elevation. INR should be closely monitored when antibiotics are started in patients on warfarin.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Anticoagulants — Warfarin','hard','mcq','A patient taking warfarin develops warfarin-induced skin necrosis on day 3 of therapy. Which underlying condition most predisposes to this serious complication?','["A) Factor V Leiden mutation causing activated protein C resistance", "B) Hereditary Protein C or Protein S deficiency", "C) Antiphospholipid syndrome causing lupus anticoagulant positivity", "D) Polycythaemia vera causing hyperviscosity"]','B','Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs predominantly in patients with hereditary Protein C or Protein S deficiency. The early depletion of these short-lived anticoagulant proteins before procoagulant factors are reduced causes dermal microvascular thrombosis and skin necrosis. Management includes stopping warfarin, giving vitamin K, and administering heparin. Factor V Leiden increases clot risk but does not predispose specifically to skin necrosis.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','Which of the following direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) directly inhibits thrombin (factor IIa)?','["A) Rivaroxaban", "B) Apixaban", "C) Dabigatran", "D) Edoxaban"]','C','Dabigatran etexilate is a prodrug converted to dabigatran, a direct thrombin (factor IIa) inhibitor that reversibly binds the active site of thrombin, preventing cleavage of fibrinogen to fibrin. Rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban are all direct factor Xa inhibitors. This distinction is pharmacologically important for reversal strategies: idarucizumab reverses dabigatran; andexanet alfa reverses Xa inhibitors.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','hard','mcq','A patient on dabigatran for non-valvular atrial fibrillation presents with life-threatening gastrointestinal bleeding. Which reversal agent should be used?','["A) Andexanet alfa — the approved reversal agent for all DOACs", "B) Protamine sulphate — reverses all heparin-like anticoagulants including dabigatran", "C) Intravenous vitamin K — rapidly reverses direct thrombin inhibitors", "D) Idarucizumab — a humanised monoclonal antibody fragment that specifically and rapidly reverses dabigatran"]','D','Idarucizumab (Praxbind) is a humanised monoclonal antibody fragment that specifically and rapidly reverses dabigatran by binding it with very high affinity (350 times greater than thrombin), displacing it and restoring haemostasis within minutes. Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors only. Protamine reverses heparin and vitamin K reverses warfarin — neither affects dabigatran.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','easy','mcq','Which DOAC requires twice-daily dosing for both non-valvular atrial fibrillation stroke prevention and VTE treatment?','["A) Apixaban", "B) Rivaroxaban", "C) Edoxaban", "D) Dabigatran (standard regimen)"]','A','Apixaban is dosed twice daily for both non-valvular AF stroke prevention (5 mg twice daily) and VTE treatment (10 mg twice daily for 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily). Rivaroxaban, edoxaban, and dabigatran are dosed once daily for AF and for VTE maintenance therapy. Understanding dosing frequency is important for patient counselling and adherence.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','In which clinical scenario are DOACs contraindicated and anticoagulation must be achieved with warfarin instead?','["A) Non-valvular atrial fibrillation with moderate CKD (eGFR 30-60 mL/min)", "B) Mechanical prosthetic heart valves", "C) Cancer-associated VTE in patients with luminal GI malignancy", "D) Deep vein thrombosis in obese patients (BMI over 40 kg/m2)"]','B','DOACs are contraindicated in patients with mechanical prosthetic heart valves because clinical trials (RE-ALIGN with dabigatran) showed higher rates of both stroke and bleeding compared with warfarin. Warfarin remains the standard of care for mechanical valves. DOACs can be used with appropriate monitoring in non-valvular AF with CKD, cancer-associated VTE, and obese patients.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','Which DOAC has the highest percentage of renal excretion, making it most problematic in patients with renal impairment?','["A) Rivaroxaban (approximately 33% renal excretion)", "B) Apixaban (approximately 27% renal excretion)", "C) Dabigatran (approximately 80% renal excretion)", "D) Edoxaban (approximately 50% renal excretion)"]','C','Dabigatran is approximately 80% renally excreted unchanged, making drug accumulation and bleeding risk significantly elevated in patients with renal impairment. It is contraindicated when eGFR is below 30 mL/min (below 15 in some guidelines). The other DOACs have lower renal excretion fractions and are generally preferred in moderate renal impairment.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','hard','mcq','Andexanet alfa is approved for reversal of which anticoagulants and what is its mechanism?','["A) Dabigatran only — it binds and sequesters dabigatran molecules in plasma", "B) All DOACs including dabigatran — it inhibits their hepatic CYP3A4 metabolism", "C) Heparin and factor Xa inhibitors — it neutralises negative charge on these molecules", "D) Rivaroxaban and apixaban — it is a modified catalytically inactive factor Xa decoy that sequesters factor Xa inhibitors"]','D','Andexanet alfa is a recombinant modified human factor Xa decoy protein lacking coagulant activity. It sequesters direct factor Xa inhibitors (rivaroxaban, apixaban) with high affinity, preventing them from inhibiting endogenous factor Xa and restoring haemostasis. It does not reverse dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor), heparin, or warfarin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','What is the primary mechanism of action of rivaroxaban?','["A) Direct reversible inhibition of factor Xa, preventing cleavage of prothrombin to thrombin", "B) Direct inhibition of thrombin (factor IIa) by binding its active catalytic site", "C) Inhibition of vitamin K epoxide reductase reducing synthesis of factor Xa", "D) Inhibition of platelet P2Y12 ADP receptors preventing platelet-dependent thrombin generation"]','A','Rivaroxaban is a direct factor Xa inhibitor that reversibly and selectively binds the active site of factor Xa, preventing its cleavage of prothrombin to thrombin and interrupting both intrinsic and extrinsic coagulation pathways. Factor IIa inhibition describes dabigatran; VKORC1 inhibition describes warfarin; P2Y12 inhibition describes clopidogrel and ticagrelor.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','easy','mcq','What is a major practical advantage of DOACs over warfarin in clinical practice?','["A) DOACs can be safely used in patients with mechanical heart valves where warfarin fails", "B) DOACs do not require routine coagulation monitoring and have fewer food and drug interactions than warfarin", "C) All DOACs have a specific, immediately available reversal agent approved for clinical use", "D) DOACs are safer than warfarin during the first trimester of pregnancy"]','B','The major practical advantages of DOACs over warfarin include predictable pharmacokinetics allowing fixed dosing without routine INR monitoring, fewer dietary (vitamin K food) and drug interactions, and faster onset of action. Specific reversal agents exist for dabigatran (idarucizumab) and Xa inhibitors (andexanet alfa) but are costly and not universally available. DOACs are contraindicated in mechanical valves and pregnancy.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','A patient on apixaban requires urgent surgery within 4 hours due to an acute abdomen. How should peri-operative anticoagulation be managed?','["A) Continue apixaban through surgery accepting the bleeding risk", "B) Stop apixaban and give protamine sulphate to reverse remaining anticoagulant activity", "C) Consider andexanet alfa for rapid reversal if surgery is life-saving and bleeding risk is high; otherwise delay surgery 24-48 hours if clinically possible", "D) Switch to warfarin immediately and proceed with surgery once INR is therapeutic"]','C','For urgent surgery in patients on apixaban, andexanet alfa can achieve rapid reversal in life-threatening situations. If surgery can be safely delayed 24-48 hours (approximately two half-lives of apixaban), drug levels will fall sufficiently without a reversal agent. Protamine does not reverse apixaban. Switching to warfarin is inappropriate in an acute emergency as warfarin takes days to reach therapeutic effect.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Direct Oral Anticoagulants','medium','mcq','Which DOACs have largely replaced LMWH for treatment of cancer-associated VTE based on recent clinical trial evidence?','["A) Dabigatran — based on the RE-COVER trial in cancer patients", "B) Edoxaban alone — based on the Hokusai VTE Cancer trial", "C) All four DOACs equally — all have equivalent evidence in cancer VTE", "D) Rivaroxaban and apixaban — based on the SELECT-D and ADAM VTE trials respectively"]','D','Rivaroxaban (SELECT-D trial) and apixaban (ADAM VTE trial) demonstrated non-inferiority or superiority to LMWH (dalteparin) for cancer-associated VTE treatment, with current ISTH and ASCO guidelines preferring these DOACs over LMWH in suitable patients. However, they carry slightly higher GI bleeding risk in luminal GI or urothelial cancers where LMWH may still be preferred.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','easy','mcq','What is the mechanism of antiplatelet action of aspirin?','["A) Irreversibly acetylates cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1), permanently inhibiting thromboxane A2 synthesis in platelets for their entire lifespan", "B) Reversibly inhibits the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets", "C) Blocks glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors preventing fibrinogen binding to activated platelets", "D) Inhibits phosphodiesterase increasing platelet cAMP and reducing platelet activation"]','A','Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1, permanently inhibiting thromboxane A2 (TXA2) synthesis. Since platelets are anucleate and cannot synthesise new COX-1, this effect lasts the entire lifespan of the platelet (7-10 days). Clopidogrel and ticagrelor block P2Y12; abciximab and eptifibatide block GPIIb/IIIa; dipyridamole inhibits phosphodiesterase.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','medium','mcq','A patient undergoes PCI with drug-eluting stent insertion for NSTEMI. Which antiplatelet regimen is most appropriate post-procedure?','["A) Aspirin monotherapy 75 mg daily for 12 months", "B) Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) with aspirin plus a P2Y12 inhibitor (e.g., ticagrelor or prasugrel) for at least 12 months", "C) Warfarin plus aspirin — to provide both antiplatelet and anticoagulant protection", "D) Clopidogrel monotherapy 75 mg daily for 6 months"]','B','Following PCI with drug-eluting stent for ACS, DAPT with aspirin plus a potent P2Y12 inhibitor (ticagrelor or prasugrel preferred over clopidogrel in ACS) is recommended for 12 months to prevent in-stent thrombosis. Aspirin monotherapy is insufficient. Warfarin plus aspirin markedly increases bleeding without adequate antiplatelet effect for stent thrombosis prevention.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','medium','mcq','Clopidogrel is a prodrug. Which cytochrome P450 enzyme is primarily responsible for converting clopidogrel to its active thiol metabolite?','["A) CYP2D6", "B) CYP3A4", "C) CYP2C19", "D) CYP1A2"]','C','Clopidogrel requires two-step hepatic bioactivation predominantly by CYP2C19 to produce its active thiol metabolite, which irreversibly blocks the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets. Patients who are CYP2C19 poor metabolisers (approximately 30% of population) have reduced activation and suboptimal platelet inhibition. Proton pump inhibitors (especially omeprazole) inhibit CYP2C19 and reduce clopidogrel efficacy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','hard','mcq','A patient with ACS on ticagrelor develops significant dyspnoea and bronchospasm attributed to the drug. Which alternative P2Y12 inhibitor should be considered?','["A) Dipyridamole monotherapy instead of a P2Y12 inhibitor", "B) High-dose clopidogrel 150 mg daily as an alternative P2Y12 inhibitor", "C) Continue ticagrelor at a lower dose to reduce the dyspnoea side effect", "D) Prasugrel if the patient has no prior history of stroke or TIA and is not elderly or low body weight"]','D','Ticagrelor causes dyspnoea in approximately 15% of patients via adenosine accumulation (it also blocks adenosine reuptake transporter). Prasugrel is an irreversible P2Y12 inhibitor and an appropriate ACS alternative, but is contraindicated in patients with prior stroke or TIA and used with caution in those aged 75 years or over, or weighing less than 60 kg, due to excess bleeding risk. Clopidogrel is an option but is less potent than ticagrelor or prasugrel.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','easy','mcq','How does ticagrelor differ from clopidogrel in its pharmacodynamic mechanism?','["A) Ticagrelor reversibly and directly binds the P2Y12 receptor without requiring hepatic bioactivation, unlike clopidogrel which is an irreversible prodrug requiring CYP2C19 activation", "B) Ticagrelor requires hepatic activation to its active form similar to clopidogrel", "C) Ticagrelor blocks COX-1 in addition to P2Y12, giving it dual antiplatelet action", "D) Ticagrelor has a much longer duration of antiplatelet effect than clopidogrel because it is irreversible"]','A','Ticagrelor (a cyclopentyltriazolopyrimidine) reversibly and directly binds the P2Y12 ADP receptor without requiring hepatic bioactivation, giving it faster onset and more predictable pharmacodynamics. Clopidogrel is a prodrug requiring CYP2C19 activation and irreversibly blocks P2Y12. Because ticagrelor is reversible, its antiplatelet effect wears off faster after stopping (24-72 hours) than clopidogrel (7-10 days).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','medium','mcq','All patients on dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) should receive which medication to reduce gastrointestinal bleeding risk?','["A) Histamine H2 receptor antagonist (ranitidine)", "B) Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) such as lansoprazole or pantoprazole", "C) Sucralfate coating the gastric mucosa", "D) Misoprostol (prostaglandin E1 analogue)"]','B','All patients on DAPT (or aspirin plus anticoagulant) should receive a PPI to reduce aspirin-related gastric mucosal injury and GI bleeding risk. Pantoprazole or lansoprazole are preferred over omeprazole in clopidogrel-treated patients as they have less CYP2C19 inhibition and therefore less attenuation of clopidogrel activation.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','medium','mcq','Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors (e.g., abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban) are used in which specific clinical setting?','["A) Long-term oral secondary prevention following myocardial infarction", "B) Primary prevention of stroke in patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation", "C) Periprocedural intravenous antiplatelet therapy during high-risk PCI in selected patients with ACS", "D) Treatment of immune thrombocytopenic purpura refractory to corticosteroids"]','C','GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors block the final common pathway of platelet aggregation by preventing fibrinogen binding to activated platelets. They are used intravenously periprocedurally during high-risk PCI in selected ACS patients, typically when thrombus burden is high. They are not used for long-term oral secondary prevention, AF anticoagulation, or ITP.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','medium','mcq','Dipyridamole is used in combination with low-dose aspirin (as Aggrenox) for secondary stroke prevention. What is its antiplatelet mechanism?','["A) Irreversibly inhibits COX-1 to block thromboxane A2 synthesis", "B) Directly and irreversibly blocks the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets", "C) Inhibits thrombin generation by binding factor Xa selectively", "D) Inhibits phosphodiesterase (preventing cAMP breakdown) and blocks adenosine reuptake, both elevating platelet cAMP and reducing platelet activation"]','D','Dipyridamole inhibits phosphodiesterase (preventing cAMP breakdown) and blocks adenosine reuptake into red cells and platelets, both leading to elevated intracellular cAMP in platelets, which inhibits platelet activation and aggregation. It is used with aspirin (Aggrenox) for secondary stroke prevention and has vasodilatory effects. It does not block COX-1, P2Y12, or factor Xa.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','easy','mcq','Why is low-dose aspirin (75-100 mg) preferred over high-dose aspirin (over 1 g/day) for long-term antiplatelet therapy?','["A) High-dose aspirin also inhibits COX-2 in vascular endothelial cells, reducing prostacyclin (PGI2) synthesis which is antithrombotic, partially counteracting its antiplatelet benefit", "B) High-dose aspirin is renally excreted before reaching platelets and has no antiplatelet effect", "C) High-dose aspirin induces P-glycoprotein which removes it from platelets before it can act", "D) High-dose aspirin saturates COX-1 excessively causing paradoxical platelet activation"]','A','At low doses (75-100 mg), aspirin selectively inhibits platelet COX-1 and TXA2. At high doses, aspirin also inhibits vascular endothelial COX-2, reducing prostacyclin (PGI2) synthesis. PGI2 is an important antithrombotic and vasodilatory prostaglandin; its reduction partially counteracts the antiplatelet benefit from TXA2 inhibition. This is why low-dose aspirin is the antiplatelet standard.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Antiplatelets','hard','mcq','A 62-year-old patient with ischaemic stroke is found to be a CYP2C19 poor metaboliser. Which antiplatelet strategy is most pharmacologically appropriate for secondary prevention?','["A) Clopidogrel 75 mg daily at standard dose — CYP2C19 status is not clinically relevant", "B) Ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily or aspirin plus extended-release dipyridamole, both of which bypass CYP2C19 dependency", "C) High-dose clopidogrel 300 mg daily to overcome the CYP2C19 deficiency", "D) Aspirin monotherapy alone is sufficient for secondary stroke prevention in CYP2C19 poor metabolisers"]','B','In CYP2C19 poor metabolisers, clopidogrel has markedly reduced antiplatelet efficacy due to impaired bioactivation. Ticagrelor does not require CYP2C19 activation and provides reliable P2Y12 inhibition. Aspirin plus extended-release dipyridamole (Aggrenox) is also guideline-supported for secondary stroke prevention. High-dose clopidogrel does not adequately overcome CYP2C19 poor metaboliser status.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','What is the mechanism of action of alteplase (recombinant tissue plasminogen activator) as a thrombolytic agent?','["A) Directly degrades fibrin by its own intrinsic proteolytic activity without requiring plasminogen", "B) Inhibits thrombin to prevent further clot propagation and allows spontaneous fibrinolysis", "C) Binds fibrin within a thrombus and preferentially converts fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin, which then lyses the clot", "D) Activates antithrombin III to dissolve established venous and arterial thrombus"]','C','Alteplase (tPA) binds to fibrin within a thrombus and converts fibrin-bound plasminogen to plasmin in a preferentially fibrin-specific manner. Plasmin then cleaves fibrin, dissolving the clot. Systemic plasminogen activation is limited at therapeutic doses. Alteplase does not directly degrade fibrin, inhibit thrombin, or activate antithrombin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','hard','mcq','A 72-year-old patient presents with acute ischaemic stroke. CT brain shows no haemorrhage. Onset was 3.5 hours ago. Which statement about thrombolytic therapy is correct?','["A) Alteplase is absolutely contraindicated beyond 3 hours from onset of acute ischaemic stroke", "B) Streptokinase is preferred in acute ischaemic stroke due to its lower cost and comparable efficacy to alteplase", "C) Thrombolysis requires blood pressure to be normalised below 120/80 mmHg before it can be administered", "D) Alteplase can be given within 4.5 hours of clearly defined symptom onset in eligible patients and is the only approved thrombolytic for ischaemic stroke"]','D','Alteplase (tPA) is the standard thrombolytic for acute ischaemic stroke within 4.5 hours of clearly defined symptom onset in eligible patients without contraindications. Streptokinase is not used in stroke due to higher intracranial haemorrhage rates. Blood pressure must be controlled to below 185/110 mmHg before tPA, not normalised to 120/80 mmHg. The treatment window was extended from 3 to 4.5 hours based on the ECASS III trial.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','easy','mcq','Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to systemic thrombolytic therapy?','["A) Recent intracranial haemorrhage or active internal bleeding", "B) Age over 75 years", "C) Uncontrolled hypertension with systolic blood pressure between 160 and 180 mmHg", "D) Diabetes mellitus with diabetic retinopathy"]','A','Recent intracranial haemorrhage and active internal bleeding are absolute contraindications to thrombolytic therapy due to the risk of catastrophic life-threatening haemorrhage. Age over 75 years is a relative (not absolute) contraindication. Uncontrolled hypertension that can be treated down to below 185/110 mmHg is a relative contraindication. Diabetes and diabetic retinopathy are relative contraindications only.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','Why cannot streptokinase be re-administered within 6-12 months of a previous dose?','["A) Streptokinase causes permanent downregulation of plasminogen receptors lasting 6 months", "B) The body develops persistent neutralising anti-streptokinase antibodies that render re-administration ineffective and increase anaphylaxis risk", "C) Streptokinase permanently depletes all circulating plasminogen and fibrinogen for 12 months", "D) Repeated streptokinase invariably causes anaphylactic shock in all patients regardless of prior tolerance"]','B','Streptokinase, derived from streptococcal bacteria, is immunogenic and induces neutralising antibodies that persist for 6-12 months. Re-administration during this period is ineffective (antibodies neutralise the drug before it can act) and carries increased risk of severe allergic or anaphylactic reactions. Alteplase (tPA) is preferred in patients with prior streptokinase exposure within 12 months.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','Tenecteplase (TNK-tPA) is increasingly preferred over alteplase for STEMI thrombolysis. What pharmacokinetic advantage enables single-bolus dosing?','["A) Tenecteplase is orally bioavailable unlike alteplase which must be given intravenously", "B) Tenecteplase does not require any adjunctive anticoagulation bridging unlike alteplase", "C) Tenecteplase has a longer half-life (approximately 20 minutes versus 5 minutes for alteplase) due to molecular modifications, enabling a single IV weight-adjusted bolus", "D) Tenecteplase carries zero risk of intracranial haemorrhage due to its complete fibrin selectivity"]','C','Tenecteplase has a substantially longer half-life than alteplase due to modifications that increase fibrin specificity, reduce PAI-1 binding, and decrease clearance. This allows weight-adjusted single IV bolus administration, which is particularly convenient for pre-hospital STEMI thrombolysis (facilitated PCI). All thrombolytics carry haemorrhagic risk and require adjunctive anticoagulation.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','hard','mcq','A patient with massive pulmonary embolism presents with haemodynamic instability (systolic BP 80 mmHg), RV dysfunction on echo, and no bleeding contraindications. What is the most appropriate treatment?','["A) Full anticoagulation alone with intravenous unfractionated heparin infusion", "B) Catheter-directed thrombolysis as the sole first-line intervention", "C) Immediate surgical embolectomy as first-line treatment before any pharmacological therapy", "D) Systemic thrombolysis with alteplase 100 mg intravenously over 2 hours plus heparin"]','D','In massive (high-risk) PE with haemodynamic instability and RV failure, systemic thrombolysis with alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours is the treatment of choice when there are no contraindications. Anticoagulation alone is insufficient and too slow in haemodynamically unstable massive PE. Catheter-directed therapy and surgical embolectomy are rescue options when systemic thrombolysis is contraindicated or has failed.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','Which of the following thrombolytics is derived from Streptococcus bacteria and acts as an indirect plasminogen activator by forming a complex with plasminogen?','["A) Streptokinase", "B) Alteplase", "C) Reteplase", "D) Tenecteplase"]','A','Streptokinase is derived from group C beta-haemolytic streptococci and acts indirectly: it forms an equimolar complex with plasminogen that acquires plasminogen activator activity, converting other plasminogen molecules to plasmin in a non-fibrin-selective systemic manner. Alteplase, reteplase, and tenecteplase are all recombinant tPA derivatives that act more selectively on fibrin-bound plasminogen.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','easy','mcq','What is the most feared and potentially devastating complication of systemic thrombolytic therapy?','["A) Severe hypotension during drug infusion", "B) Intracranial haemorrhage (ICH)", "C) Severe allergic or anaphylactoid reaction", "D) Acute renal failure from myoglobinuria"]','B','Intracranial haemorrhage (ICH) is the most feared complication of systemic thrombolytics, occurring in approximately 0.6-1% of patients treated for acute ischaemic stroke and is often fatal or causes severe permanent disability. Hypotension and allergy (particularly with streptokinase) occur but are less devastating. Renal failure is not a characteristic complication of thrombolytics.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','A 58-year-old man with STEMI cannot receive primary PCI within 120 minutes. Which thrombolytic is recommended and with what adjunctive anticoagulation?','["A) Streptokinase alone without adjunctive anticoagulation to avoid excess bleeding", "B) Alteplase alone — no adjunctive anticoagulation is needed as alteplase is fully fibrin-selective", "C) Fibrin-specific agent (tenecteplase or alteplase) plus adjunctive enoxaparin or unfractionated heparin to prevent reocclusion", "D) Urokinase with oral clopidogrel loading — the preferred combination for pharmacoinvasive strategy"]','C','Current STEMI guidelines recommend fibrin-specific thrombolytics (tenecteplase or alteplase) when primary PCI is unavailable within 120 minutes, with mandatory adjunctive anticoagulation (enoxaparin preferred over UFH for tenecteplase) to prevent reocclusion after successful thrombolysis. Streptokinase is now rarely used. Urokinase is not a recommended STEMI thrombolytic.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Thrombolytics','medium','mcq','A patient treated with streptokinase for DVT develops hypotension, fever, and urticaria during the infusion. What is the most likely cause?','["A) Pulmonary embolism occurring during DVT lysis causing haemodynamic compromise", "B) Fibrinogen depletion causing capillary leak syndrome and systemic vasodilation", "C) Massive haemolysis triggered by streptokinase binding to red cell surface antigens", "D) Immune-mediated allergic or anaphylactoid reaction to the bacterial-derived streptokinase protein"]','D','Streptokinase is a bacterial-derived protein and is immunogenic, causing allergic or anaphylactoid reactions (fever, rigors, urticaria, hypotension) in approximately 3-5% of patients, especially those with recent streptococcal infection and pre-existing anti-streptokinase antibodies. Management includes stopping the infusion and giving antihistamines, corticosteroids, and adrenaline for anaphylaxis. This is distinct from haemorrhagic complications.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','easy','mcq','Which class of chemotherapy agents works primarily by crosslinking DNA strands, preventing replication and transcription?','["A) Alkylating agents (e.g., cyclophosphamide, melphalan, cisplatin)", "B) Antimetabolites (e.g., methotrexate, cytarabine)", "C) Topoisomerase inhibitors (e.g., etoposide, irinotecan)", "D) Vinca alkaloids (e.g., vincristine, vinblastine)"]','A','Alkylating agents form covalent bonds with DNA nucleotides (particularly guanine N7), creating intra- and interstrand crosslinks that prevent DNA replication and transcription, triggering apoptosis. They are cell-cycle non-specific. Antimetabolites are structural analogues of nucleotides; topoisomerase inhibitors trap topoisomerases creating DNA breaks; vinca alkaloids disrupt the mitotic spindle.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','medium','mcq','Cyclophosphamide requires hepatic activation. Which metabolite is responsible for its characteristic bladder toxicity (haemorrhagic cystitis)?','["A) Phosphoramide mustard — the active cytotoxic metabolite", "B) Acrolein — a toxic metabolite that accumulates in the bladder urothelium causing haemorrhagic cystitis", "C) Hydroxycyclophosphamide — which directly damages the bladder mucosa", "D) 4-ketocyclophosphamide — which forms crosslinks specifically in bladder epithelial cells"]','B','Cyclophosphamide is metabolised by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4 to active phosphoramide mustard (the cytotoxic alkylating metabolite) and acrolein (a bladder toxin). Acrolein accumulates in urine and causes haemorrhagic cystitis. This is prevented by adequate hydration and co-administration of mesna, which binds and detoxifies acrolein in the urine without affecting the antitumour activity of phosphoramide mustard.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','medium','mcq','Methotrexate is an antimetabolite that competitively inhibits which enzyme critical to folate metabolism?','["A) Thymidylate synthase — blocking dTMP synthesis", "B) Ribonucleotide reductase — blocking DNA precursor synthesis", "C) Dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) — preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to active tetrahydrofolate", "D) Topoisomerase II — causing DNA double-strand breaks in tumour cells"]','C','Methotrexate is a folate antimetabolite that competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate (THF). Without THF, synthesis of thymidylate and purines (required for DNA synthesis) is halted, blocking cell division. Folinic acid (leucovorin) rescues normal cells. Thymidylate synthase is inhibited by 5-FU; topoisomerase II by etoposide and doxorubicin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','hard','mcq','Which topoisomerase inhibitor used in haematological malignancies is most associated with secondary acute myeloid leukaemia as a late complication?','["A) Irinotecan (topoisomerase I inhibitor)", "B) Topotecan (topoisomerase I inhibitor)", "C) Vincristine (microtubule inhibitor)", "D) Etoposide (topoisomerase II inhibitor)"]','D','Etoposide inhibits topoisomerase II by stabilising the cleavage complex, causing DNA double-strand breaks. Its use is associated with secondary AML (typically with MLL gene rearrangements at chromosome 11q23), usually presenting 2-3 years after treatment. This represents a specific class effect of topoisomerase II poisons. Topoisomerase I inhibitors (irinotecan, topotecan) and vincristine carry different secondary malignancy profiles.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','easy','mcq','Vinca alkaloids (e.g., vincristine, vinblastine) exert their anticancer effects through which mechanism?','["A) Bind to beta-tubulin and inhibit microtubule polymerisation, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase (M phase)", "B) Intercalate into DNA and inhibit topoisomerase II, causing DNA double-strand breaks", "C) Inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, blocking nucleotide synthesis required for DNA replication", "D) Form reactive intermediates that alkylate DNA guanine residues causing interstrand crosslinks"]','A','Vinca alkaloids bind to beta-tubulin (at a site distinct from taxanes) and prevent tubulin polymerisation into microtubules, disrupting formation of the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in M phase (metaphase). They are M-phase specific agents. Taxanes (paclitaxel) also target tubulin but stabilise rather than depolymerise microtubules. Intercalation and alkylation describe other drug classes.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','medium','mcq','Which antimetabolite is used as the backbone of induction chemotherapy for acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) and acts as a cytidine analogue that inhibits DNA polymerase alpha?','["A) 6-Mercaptopurine — used in ALL maintenance therapy", "B) Cytarabine (Ara-C) — incorporated into DNA after phosphorylation and inhibits DNA polymerase, blocking DNA chain elongation", "C) Gemcitabine — a deoxycytidine analogue used primarily in solid tumours", "D) Cladribine — a purine analogue used primarily in hairy cell leukaemia"]','B','Cytarabine (Ara-C) is a cytidine analogue phosphorylated intracellularly to Ara-CTP, which competes with dCTP for incorporation into DNA and inhibits DNA polymerase alpha. It is S-phase specific and the cornerstone of AML induction (e.g., "7+3" regimen with an anthracycline). 6-MP is used in ALL maintenance; cladribine in hairy cell leukaemia; gemcitabine mainly in pancreatic and lung cancers.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','hard','mcq','Anthracyclines (doxorubicin, daunorubicin) are widely used in haematological malignancies. What is the cumulative dose-limiting toxicity that restricts their long-term use?','["A) Peripheral neuropathy causing progressive sensorimotor deficits", "B) Nephrotoxicity (renal tubular acidosis and electrolyte wasting)", "C) Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy) due to free radical generation in cardiomyocytes", "D) Pulmonary fibrosis from direct alveolar epithelial toxicity"]','C','Anthracyclines cause cumulative dose-dependent cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy) due to free radical generation via iron-mediated redox cycling and topoisomerase II-beta inhibition in cardiomyocytes. Doxorubicin has a lifetime cumulative dose limit of approximately 450-550 mg/m2. Dexrazoxane (iron chelator) provides cardioprotection. Pulmonary fibrosis is from bleomycin; peripheral neuropathy from vinca alkaloids; nephrotoxicity from cisplatin.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','medium','mcq','5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) exerts its primary cytotoxic mechanism through which action?','["A) DNA interstrand crosslinking by its reactive fluorine atom", "B) Binding tubulin and inhibiting microtubule polymerisation in tumour cells", "C) Intercalating into DNA and inhibiting topoisomerase I", "D) Inhibition of thymidylate synthase (via FdUMP), blocking dTMP synthesis and depleting the DNA precursor dTTP"]','D','5-FU is converted intracellularly to FdUMP, which forms an irreversible ternary complex with thymidylate synthase (TS) and the cofactor 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking synthesis of dTMP (thymidine monophosphate). This depletes the dTTP pool required for DNA synthesis, causing cell death. Leucovorin is given with 5-FU to stabilise the ternary complex and enhance its inhibitory effect.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','easy','mcq','What is the purpose of leucovorin (folinic acid) rescue therapy given after high-dose methotrexate administration?','["A) To rescue normal rapidly dividing cells by providing pre-reduced folate that bypasses the DHFR step blocked by methotrexate", "B) To enhance the anticancer efficacy of methotrexate by increasing drug uptake into tumour cells", "C) To prevent methotrexate-induced renal failure by chelating the drug in the tubular lumen", "D) To stimulate megakaryocyte differentiation and restore platelet counts after chemotherapy-induced thrombocytopenia"]','A','Leucovorin (folinic acid) is a pre-reduced folate (5-formyltetrahydrofolate) that bypasses the DHFR step, allowing normal cells (GI mucosa, bone marrow) to resume DNA synthesis even when DHFR is inhibited by methotrexate. It is given at a precisely timed interval to allow adequate tumour exposure to methotrexate before rescue. It does not chelate methotrexate or stimulate platelet production.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Chemotherapy Principles','hard','mcq','Bleomycin is a component of ABVD chemotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma. What is the dose-limiting toxicity that requires careful monitoring with pulmonary function tests?','["A) Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy) requiring echocardiography monitoring", "B) Pulmonary toxicity (interstitial pneumonitis and progressive fibrosis) occurring in 10-20% of patients", "C) Haemorrhagic cystitis due to reactive metabolite accumulation in the bladder", "D) Cumulative nephrotoxicity manifesting as renal tubular acidosis and hypomagnesaemia"]','B','Bleomycin causes pulmonary toxicity (interstitial pneumonitis progressing to fibrosis) as its dose-limiting adverse effect, occurring in 10-20% of patients, particularly at cumulative doses above 400 units. It generates free radicals causing oxidative DNA strand breaks and lung injury, exacerbated by high inspired oxygen concentrations. DLCO is monitored during treatment. Cardiotoxicity is from anthracyclines; haemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide; nephrotoxicity from cisplatin.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','Hydroxyurea is used in sickle cell disease. What is its primary mechanism of benefit in reducing painful vaso-occlusive crises?','["A) It prevents sickling by chelating intracellular iron within haemoglobin S molecules", "B) It blocks the interaction between polymerised HbS and the red cell membrane cytoskeleton", "C) It inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and increases fetal haemoglobin (HbF) production, which dilutes HbS and inhibits its polymerisation", "D) It stimulates accelerated erythropoiesis to replace sickled cells with healthier red cells rapidly"]','C','Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and induces HbF synthesis (through p38 MAPK and guanylyl cyclase activation). Elevated HbF (gamma-globin chains) dilutes HbS, interfering with HbS polymer formation and reducing sickling frequency. It also decreases adhesion molecules and nitric oxide scavenging. Clinical benefits include fewer painful crises, less ACS, and reduced transfusion requirements.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','hard','mcq','Imatinib revolutionised the treatment of chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML). What is its specific molecular target?','["A) CD20 on B-lymphocyte cell surface — causing direct apoptosis of CML blasts", "B) Topoisomerase II within CML blast cells — preventing DNA strand re-ligation", "C) VEGF receptor on CML stromal cells — blocking leukaemia-supporting angiogenesis", "D) BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase — the constitutively active fusion kinase produced by the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) translocation"]','D','Imatinib selectively inhibits the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase, the constitutively active fusion protein from the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) translocation, by competitively occupying its ATP-binding site. This blocks downstream proliferative and anti-apoptotic signalling driving CML. CD20 is targeted by rituximab; topoisomerase II by etoposide/doxorubicin; VEGF receptors by bevacizumab/sunitinib.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','easy','mcq','Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in NHL and ITP. What surface antigen does it target?','["A) CD20 on B-lymphocytes (including malignant B-cells and autoreactive B-cells in ITP)", "B) CD3 on T-lymphocytes causing T-cell depletion", "C) CD33 on myeloid blasts in AML", "D) HER2/neu receptor overexpressed on certain lymphoma cell lines"]','A','Rituximab is a chimeric anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody targeting CD20, a transmembrane phosphoprotein expressed on pre-B and mature B-lymphocytes but not plasma cells. Its mechanisms include complement-dependent cytotoxicity (CDC), antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC), and direct apoptosis induction. In ITP, it depletes autoreactive B-cells that produce antiplatelet antibodies.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','Dexamethasone is a key component of multiple myeloma treatment regimens. What is its primary anti-myeloma mechanism?','["A) It inhibits the 26S proteasome causing accumulation of misfolded immunoglobulin proteins in myeloma cells", "B) It binds glucocorticoid receptors in myeloma plasma cells, activating pro-apoptotic pathways (BIM upregulation) and suppressing pro-survival cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha)", "C) It directly inhibits angiogenesis in the bone marrow microenvironment by blocking VEGF", "D) It enhances complement-mediated lysis of CD38-expressing plasma cells"]','B','Dexamethasone binds glucocorticoid receptors in myeloma plasma cells, activating pro-apoptotic pathways (BIM upregulation, Bcl-2/Bcl-XL downregulation) and suppressing cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha) that support myeloma survival and osteoclast activation. Proteasome inhibition describes bortezomib; VEGF/angiogenesis inhibition describes thalidomide and lenalidomide; CD38 targeting describes daratumumab.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','A CML patient achieves complete cytogenetic response on imatinib but relapses. BCR-ABL kinase domain analysis reveals a T315I gatekeeper mutation. Which TKI is active against this specific mutation?','["A) Dasatinib — a second-generation TKI with broader BCR-ABL inhibitor activity", "B) Nilotinib — a potent second-generation BCR-ABL inhibitor with improved potency over imatinib", "C) Ponatinib — a third-generation TKI specifically designed to overcome the T315I gatekeeper mutation", "D) Bosutinib — a dual SRC/ABL inhibitor active against most imatinib-resistant mutations"]','C','The T315I gatekeeper mutation abolishes the threonine hydroxyl group that forms a critical hydrogen bond required by all first- and second-generation TKIs (imatinib, dasatinib, nilotinib, bosutinib). Ponatinib is a third-generation TKI engineered with a carbon-carbon triple bond that bypasses the T315I steric clash and maintains BCR-ABL inhibition. It is the treatment of choice for T315I-mutant CML.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','hard','mcq','A patient with ITP refractory to corticosteroids and rituximab is started on eltrombopag. What is the mechanism of action of eltrombopag?','["A) It binds Fc gamma receptors on splenic macrophages, preventing macrophage-mediated platelet destruction", "B) It depletes autoreactive plasma cells producing antiplatelet antibodies", "C) It inhibits complement-mediated platelet lysis by blocking C3 convertase", "D) It binds and activates the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl) on megakaryocyte progenitors, stimulating platelet production"]','D','Eltrombopag (and romiplostim) are thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RA) that bind and activate c-Mpl (the TPO receptor) on megakaryocyte progenitors, stimulating megakaryocyte proliferation and differentiation to increase platelet production. They do not deplete autoantibodies or block Fc receptors. IV immunoglobulin blocks Fcgamma receptors to temporarily prevent platelet destruction.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','Hydroxyurea is used in polycythaemia vera to reduce thrombosis risk. Which blood count parameter is the primary therapeutic target?','["A) Reduction of haematocrit to below 45% in males and 42% in females to reduce hyperviscosity-related thrombosis", "B) Reduction of white cell count to below 4 × 10 to the power of 9 per litre", "C) Normalisation of haemoglobin to 12-13 g/dL", "D) Reduction of ESR to within normal range as a marker of disease activity"]','A','In polycythaemia vera, haematocrit reduction to below 45% (males) and 42% (females) is the primary therapeutic target, as elevated haematocrit directly drives hyperviscosity and thrombosis risk. The CYTO-PV trial confirmed that tight haematocrit control reduces thrombotic events. Hydroxyurea also reduces markedly elevated platelet and WBC counts as secondary benefits. ESR is not a primary PV therapeutic target.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','Bortezomib is a key agent in multiple myeloma treatment. What is its mechanism of action?','["A) It inhibits CD38 on myeloma plasma cells causing antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity", "B) It reversibly inhibits the 26S proteasome chymotryptic-like activity, causing accumulation of polyubiquitinated proteins and triggering apoptosis in protein-secreting myeloma cells", "C) It blocks VEGF to prevent angiogenesis in the bone marrow niche supporting myeloma", "D) It alkylates DNA in myeloma plasma cells forming interstrand crosslinks similar to melphalan"]','B','Bortezomib is a boronic acid proteasome inhibitor that reversibly inhibits the 26S proteasome beta5 subunit chymotryptic-like activity. This prevents degradation of polyubiquitinated proteins (including IkB, p53, and misfolded immunoglobulins), triggering the unfolded protein response and apoptosis — particularly in secretory plasma cells with high protein turnover. Daratumumab targets CD38; bevacizumab targets VEGF; melphalan is an alkylator.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','hard','mcq','A 45-year-old patient with CML in chronic phase shows inadequate molecular response after 6 months of imatinib (BCR-ABL transcripts not declining adequately). What is the recommended management?','["A) Immediately proceed to allogeneic stem cell transplantation — this is the only curative option", "B) Add hydroxyurea to suppress the white cell count while continuing imatinib", "C) Assess adherence and check for CYP3A4 drug interactions, then consider switching to a second-generation TKI (dasatinib or nilotinib) if response remains suboptimal", "D) Double the imatinib dose to 800 mg daily indefinitely without further assessment"]','C','When CML patients show suboptimal molecular response to imatinib, the first step is to assess adherence (a major cause of failure) and check for drug interactions (CYP3A4 inducers reduce imatinib levels). If response remains suboptimal after optimising adherence, switching to dasatinib or nilotinib is recommended as they achieve deeper molecular responses more rapidly. Allogeneic SCT is reserved for accelerated or blast phase, or multiple TKI failures.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed')),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Pharmacology','Haematological Disease-Specific Drugs','medium','mcq','Rituximab infusion commonly causes a first-dose reaction characterised by fever, rigors, hypotension, and urticaria. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?','["A) Classical IgE-mediated anaphylaxis requiring prior sensitisation and mast cell degranulation", "B) Complement activation causing immune complex deposition in dermal capillaries", "C) Direct mast cell degranulation by the Fc portion of rituximab independent of antigen binding", "D) Cytokine release syndrome (CRS) caused by rapid lysis of CD20-positive B-cells and liberation of TNF-alpha, IL-6, and other cytokines"]','D','Rituximab infusion reactions are primarily due to cytokine release syndrome (CRS) — the antibody binds CD20 on circulating B-cells and activates complement and ADCC, causing rapid cell lysis and systemic release of inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6, IFN-gamma). This is why rituximab is infused slowly initially and patients are premedicated with paracetamol and antihistamines. True IgE-mediated anaphylaxis is rare and requires prior sensitisation.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Lippincott Illustrated Reviews Pharmacology 7th Ed'));
